+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and scoring
+
+This review covers the Spring Boot accounts API, its H2/JPA persistence, authentication and tenant isolation, payment
+dispatch and queuing, account closure workflow, and the scheme, fraud, ledger, and notification integrations. It is
+based on the implementation and configuration in this repository, including the fact that the production-looking
+configuration still uses an in-memory H2 database.
+
+Scores are on a 1–10 scale:
+
+- **Severity (S):** 1 = negligible inconvenience; 10 = customer money loss, material confidentiality/integrity breach,
+  or prolonged service-wide outage.
+- **Occurrence (O):** 1 = rare with current design; 10 = expected or frequent under normal operation.
+- **Detection (D):** 1 = almost certain immediate detection; 10 = unlikely to be detected before customer or dependent
+  system impact.
+
+`RPN = S × O × D`. These are relative prioritisation scores, not a claim about incident frequency. Detection assumes
+the observability visible in this repository: there is no metrics/tracing/alerting configuration and no actuator
+dependency, although `/actuator/health` is allow-listed.
+
+## FMEA register
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers | Effect on dependent systems and teams | Detection | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| F-01 | Service fails to start or becomes unavailable | Bad integration configuration, JVM/dependency failure, database failure, or application crash; no redundancy or readiness design is shown | All account, case, closure, and payment API calls fail or time out | Mobile/web channels cannot load account data; support and operations cannot work; payment intake may be unavailable | Synthetic API checks and platform process/restart alerts; `/actuator/health` is configured but actuator is not a declared dependency | 9 | 4 | 6 | 216 | Add a real liveness/readiness health endpoint with dependency checks, structured logs, metrics and alerts; deploy multiple instances with tested restart/failover and runbooks. |
+| F-02 | Persistent account/payment data is lost on restart or instance loss | `jdbc:h2:mem:accounts;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1` is process-memory storage and schema is `create-drop`; fixtures are loaded at startup | Accounts, balances, payment history, cases, and closure requests disappear or revert to fixtures; customers may see wrong balances and payment state | Ledger, scheme, support, and reconciliation teams see missing references and cannot audit or recover workflows | Customer reports, reconciliation, or startup logs; no durable-store health or backup/restore check is present | 10 | 5 | 9 | 450 | Use a durable, HA production database with migrations, encryption, backups and point-in-time recovery. Prevent fixture loading in production and test restore and data-integrity reconciliation. |
+| F-03 | Cross-tenant or over-broad data access | A future query/controller bypasses `AccessPolicy`, trusts token tenant/role claims incorrectly, or relies on IDs without tenant predicates; resource rules are distributed across services | A customer or bank employee can view another tenant’s balances, transactions, payment details, or customer PII | Regulatory breach, tenant notification, incident response, and loss of trust; downstream channels may cache or propagate leaked data | Access-control tests currently cover representative cases, but no production audit trail or cross-tenant canary is configured | 10 | 3 | 8 | 240 | Make tenant-scoped repository methods mandatory, centralise authorization tests, add deny-by-default contract tests for every endpoint, log security decisions without sensitive data, and alert on cross-tenant query/access anomalies. |
+| F-04 | Forged, expired, mis-issued, or unavailable identity tokens are accepted or all valid calls are rejected | JWT key/claim/role mapping or identity-service key rotation misconfiguration; token tenant and roles are trusted as authoritative | Either customers receive 401s and cannot access money, or an attacker gains customer/staff privileges | Identity, API gateway, support, and security teams receive an authentication incident; all dependent clients may retry and amplify load | HTTP 401/403 rate, token-validation errors, key-rotation probes, and identity integration alerts | 10 | 2 | 7 | 140 | Validate issuer/audience and claim schema, automate key rotation and clock-skew tests, monitor 401/403 by tenant/client, and maintain a rapid identity rollback/runbook. |
+| F-05 | Concurrent payments overspend an account or exceed the daily limit | `PaymentService` reads balance and today’s payments before dispatch, with no row lock, optimistic version, atomic reservation, or unique idempotency key | Duplicate/parallel requests can send more money than the balance or limit permits | Scheme receives excess instructions; ledger and finance reconciliation diverge; bank absorbs loss and support handles disputes | Balance-vs-ledger and daily-limit reconciliation; concurrency/load tests; current API has no explicit alert | 10 | 5 | 8 | 400 | Introduce an idempotency key, atomic payment reservation and database locking/versioning; enforce balance/limit in one transaction and test concurrent requests at realistic load. |
+| F-06 | Duplicate external payment after client retry or ambiguous response | `POST` creates a new payment each time; scheme submission has no idempotency contract. A timeout can occur after the scheme accepted the instruction | Customer may pay the same payee twice or see an apparently failed payment later complete | Scheme and ledger contain duplicates; operations need manual recall/reconciliation and customer support faces complaints | Compare scheme instructions and internal references; customer reports; no duplicate-payment metric or reconciliation job is present | 10 | 4 | 9 | 360 | Require an idempotency key and persist/request the same end-to-end reference; make scheme calls idempotent, reconcile unknown outcomes, and expose a safe pending state rather than inviting blind retry. |
+| F-07 | Scheme timeout/error leaves payment outcome and local state inconsistent | `SchemeTimeoutException` is not handled by `PaymentDispatcher`; HTTP errors other than client rejection are not normalised. The scheme may accept before the response is lost | API returns 5xx or payment remains/rolls back as `RECEIVED`; customer cannot tell whether to retry | Scheme may have an instruction the accounts service cannot find; operations must investigate before releasing/retrying funds | 5xx/error-rate and scheme-reference reconciliation; current code only logs ledger and notification failures, not scheme timeout handling | 10 | 4 | 9 | 360 | Catch and classify transport failures, persist `SUBMISSION_UNKNOWN`, query/reconcile by idempotent reference, and alert on aged unknown payments. Add contract tests for fail-before and fail-after acceptance. |
+| F-08 | Scheme payment is accepted but ledger posting is missing while local balance is debited | `PaymentDispatcher` debits the JPA account before posting; on `LedgerTimeoutException` it stores `POSTING_FAILED` but does not retry or compensate. A non-timeout ledger error can also abort the request after scheme acceptance | Payment may leave the account through the scheme while the displayed balance is wrong; customer may be unable to understand or recover it | Core ledger, scheme, finance, and reconciliation records diverge; manual repair and possible financial loss | `POSTING_FAILED` records and balance/ledger reconciliation; no scheduled retry, alert, or repair workflow is implemented | 10 | 4 | 8 | 320 | Use an outbox/state machine with durable retry and idempotent ledger reference; do not treat a timeout as a final state without reconciliation. Add a compensating/hold process and alert on any aged posting failure. |
+| F-09 | Fraud screening is bypassed | Fraud client and decisions exist, but the payment creation/dispatch path never calls `FraudScreeningClient` | A fraudulent payment can be sent without screening, referral, or hold; legitimate customers may also lack expected protection | Fraud, compliance, and risk teams lose a control and evidence trail; scheme and ledger process unsafe instructions | Fraud-loss monitoring or code review; no payment-path metric or fraud decision is recorded | 10 | 6 | 10 | 600 | Put screening before scheme submission, persist ACCEPT/REFER/REJECT and policy version, fail closed or use an explicitly approved degraded mode, and alert on payments without a decision. |
+| F-10 | Queued payment is not executed on its due date | Scheduler disabled, instance unavailable at 06:00, timezone/cutoff defect, or dispatcher failure; job has no retry/backoff or claim/lease | Customer’s payment is delayed without a reliable ETA; repeated manual retries can create duplicate risk | Scheme receives late instructions; support, payments operations, and finance must chase aged `QUEUED` rows | Metric/alert for queued age and due-versus-submitted count; currently only database inspection can reveal stuck rows | 8 | 5 | 8 | 320 | Replace best-effort scheduling with a durable queue/outbox, distributed lock/lease, retries and dead-letter handling; alert on due queued payments and reconcile execution dates. |
+| F-11 | Notification is not delivered after a payment | Notification call fails or times out; exception is swallowed after a warning log and there is no durable notification queue | Customer does not receive expected payment confirmation and may submit a duplicate or contact support | Notification, support, and complaints teams lose a customer-facing event; audit evidence is incomplete | Notification provider delivery reports and log aggregation; no delivery metric, retry, or customer-visible notification status | 6 | 6 | 7 | 252 | Publish notifications through a durable outbox with retry/dead-letter handling and idempotency; track delivery outcome and alert on backlog/failure rate. |
+| F-12 | Account balance diverges from the authoritative ledger | The service stores and mutates its own `Account.balance`; payment debit is local and transaction history is fixture/static data, with no inbound ledger update or reconciliation | Displayed balance and available funds are wrong; customers can make unsafe decisions or receive declines unexpectedly | Ledger/finance reports disagree with API; support cannot give a reliable statement and reconciliation becomes manual | Periodic account-to-ledger reconciliation and invariant checks; no reconciliation implementation is present | 10 | 5 | 8 | 400 | Define one balance authority, consume ledger events or read balance from the ledger, and implement continuous reconciliation with quarantine/alerting for mismatches. |
+| F-13 | Account closure workflow stalls or closes incorrectly | Only request creation and pending-list endpoints are implemented; no approve/reject action is present, and an approved fixture does not itself change `Account.status` | Customers cannot complete closure, or may believe an account is closed while it remains usable | Approvers, operations, statements, payments, and regulatory retention workflows remain stuck or inconsistent | Aged pending requests and account-status/request-status reconciliation; no SLA metric or approval endpoint exists | 8 | 7 | 9 | 504 | Implement a transactional, authorised approve/reject operation with separation of duties, atomic account state transition, payment blocking, audit events, and aged-request alerts. |
+| F-14 | Duplicate closure requests are created under concurrency | Existence check and insert are separate; there is no database uniqueness constraint for one pending request per account | Customer and approvers see conflicting requests; closure may be processed twice or require manual correction | Operations and audit records become ambiguous; downstream closure processing may run twice | Duplicate pending-request query and audit review; no uniqueness violation or metric currently exists | 7 | 4 | 8 | 224 | Add a database unique constraint for `(account_id, status)` where status is pending (or an equivalent locking/state model), and handle constraint conflicts as 409. |
+| F-15 | Internal or sensitive information is exposed through a new/incorrect response path | Notes/comments are explicitly marked staff-only, but enforcement is not shown in controllers; admin customer responses include PII and payment views include full payee account numbers | Customer or wrongly scoped staff member sees fraud, hardship, joint-holder, risk, or payment information that should be restricted | Privacy incident, regulatory reporting, customer notification, and support/security investigation | Schema/response contract tests, DLP scanning, access logs, and privacy review; current tests cover only selected endpoints | 9 | 3 | 8 | 216 | Create role-specific DTOs and repository queries that exclude internal fields by construction; add endpoint-by-role response tests, field-level logging controls, and privacy regression checks. |
+| F-16 | Time-based behavior uses inconsistent clocks | Payment flow injects a `Clock`, but `ClosureRequest` and `SupportCase` call `Instant.now()` directly; business-day logic is limited to Monday–Friday and has no holiday calendar | Payment execution, case/closure timestamps, and customer-visible dates can be inconsistent around timezone/DST; bank holidays may be mishandled | Operations and audit teams see misleading timelines; scheme submissions can be early/late relative to bank rules | Boundary tests around DST, cutoff, weekends and holidays; timestamp anomaly monitoring | 7 | 5 | 7 | 245 | Inject one configured clock everywhere, use an explicit business-calendar service, and test cutoff/DST/holiday boundaries against scheme requirements. |
+| F-17 | Unhandled integration or persistence exception returns an opaque 5xx after partial work | Exception handler maps only selected domain/validation exceptions; external client errors and repository failures are not consistently classified or correlated | Customer sees a generic failure and may retry an operation whose external side effect already occurred | On-call lacks a safe replay decision; API clients may retry aggressively and create load or duplicates | 5xx rate, exception logs with correlation IDs, and distributed traces; current logging is sparse and mostly WARN for selected paths | 8 | 6 | 7 | 336 | Add global safe error handling with correlation IDs, classify retryable versus final outcomes, preserve payment state before external effects, and publish client retry guidance. |
+
+## Priority interpretation
+
+The highest immediate engineering priorities are F-09 (fraud bypass, RPN 600), F-13 (closure workflow, RPN 504), F-02
+(ephemeral persistence, RPN 450), F-05/F-12 (balance and limit integrity, RPN 400 each), and F-06/F-07/F-10/F-17
+(ambiguous or stuck payment processing). FMEA scores should be revisited after production telemetry exists; in
+particular, occurrence and detection are currently estimates because the repository contains no operational dashboards,
+alerts, retry queues, or reconciliation jobs.
+
+Before release, require evidence for: durable database recovery; authorization and tenant-isolation tests; concurrent
+payment/idempotency tests; scheme-acceptance/timeout reconciliation; fraud decisions on every payment; ledger
+reconciliation; queued-payment retry and alerting; and an end-to-end closure approval flow.
